  "reply": "Now I have a clear picture. The current implementation is a stub that only matches `ms`. I need to build a full parser for `h|m|s|ms` components in strictly descending order with whitespace support and overflow guards.The failing tests are `'1h2m3s4ms'`, `'0h0m0s0ms'`, and `'1m4ms'` — components without spaces between them. My pattern required `\\s+` (one or more) between components, but whitespace is optional. The fix: change `\\s+` to `\\s*` in the separator group. The order-check loop still rejects duplicates/out-of-order regardless, and the full-string anchor still rejects junk.All 26 tests pass (10 accept + 16 reject).\n\n**What changed in `src/duration.ts`:**\n\nThe stub was replaced with a full implementation that:\n\n1. **Validates structure** with a single regex anchored to the full string — `^\\s*\\d+(h|ms|m|s)(\\s*\\d+(h|ms|m|s))*\\s*$`. This rejects empty input, signs, fractions, bad units (`d`), space between a number and its unit (`1 s`), and trailing junk in one shot. `ms` is listed before `m` in the alternation so it matches greedily.\n\n2. **Enforces strictly-descending unit order** (h=3 > m=2 > s=1 > ms=0) with a `lastOrder` sentinel; duplicates and out-of-order units both trigger `>= lastOrder`, throwing `RangeError`.\n\n3. **Guards overflow** at three levels: individual parsed value > `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`, that value × multiplier > `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`, and running total > `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` — covering cases like `'2501999792984h'` (contribution overflows) and `'9007199254740992ms'` (value itself overflows).",
